the idea came from mr. Boehner of Ohio  who was head of the conservative  opportunities Council and he came to me  two weeks ago when he suggested that it  might be good for the country and good  for the Congress if we had a real clash  of ideas and a real debate on some of  the most important issues facing this  country I thought it was a great idea I  went back to the members of the  Progressive Caucus they thought it was a  great idea we're ready to go what are  you saying about what is going on right  now on the floor of the house are you  basically saying there's not much debate  going on yeah well Jim when I'm I'm very  frustrated i think most members of  Congress are frustrated with how little  the american people really know I as to  what's going on in Congress or in fact  are knowledgeable about the most  important issues facing this country the  corporately owned mass media in my view  does a horrendous job we spend more time  with violence and sensationalism and  five second sound bites rather than  honest debate about the terrible crises  facing this country this country is  undergoing major economic decline our  people are becoming poorer but one of  two nations in the industrialized world  without a national health care system we  lead the world the industrialized world  among poverty for our kids etc etc etc  where is the debate how did this happen  who owns America we're not having that  debate so I think that having some  people from the right and some of us who  are progressives on the left really  clash and talk about the future of  America talk about the problems facing  this country will give the American  people an opportunity to hear serious  debate and I think that that's a real  positive thing tell us specifically when  this will happen the first debate as i  understand it will be wednesday november  third our and it will be on the  single-payer health care system  obviously I and Jim McDermott will be  participating in that debate believe  that the Canadian single-payer system is  the most cost-effective way to provide  comprehensive universal health care all  Americans I presume I think without fear  of contradiction that our conservative  Republican friends will disagree and  there will be then a vigorous debate on  the enormously important issue of  national health care in this country and  the debate will take place within the  format of special orders yeah what we're  going to do and I think it's it's the  best  thing to do is to in a sense do it  informally all that has to happen is a  member requests an hour and then that  member then we'll give it over to a  moderator who will conduct our the  debate in an agreed-upon format with  certain time for each participant you of  course are the only independent in the  US Congress and let me tell you  something the longer I'm here the more  and more proud I am of my state and  myself for that achievement yes I'm the  only independent in the house and you  lined up with John Boehner who some  would describe as fairly conservative  john is a very conservative Republican  that's right another part of the  equation over here the Democratic  leadership has been talking about what  they are saying is oxford style debate  you what's your reaction to that I don't  think it makes a damn bit of difference  whether whatever it is they've been  talking about it and talking about and  that's what goes on here for years  people talk about it it's not that  complicated John proposed a approach  which i think is sensible it's fair you  give proponents a certain amount of time  people who are in opposition a certain  amount of time and you have the debate  then these guys have been talking about  this forever and I think what I'm proud  of is that John and I are moving this  thing along a lot more rapidly and they  were thinking about would you see  yourself part being able to participate  in what they come up with later well I'm  not quite sure that the Democratic  leadership would want me to defend their  point of view my point of view is a bit  more progressive than the Democratic  leadership but I think within the  Progressive Caucus we have a number of  very very bright people who are  concerned about the major issues facing  this country we went around the room and  it'll be people pairing off on on  particular issues I myself feel very  strongly about the need for single-payer  canadian-style system obviously Vermont  borders Canada so that's an issue that I  have a familiarity with jim mcdermott  request as the author of the major  single-payer bill in the house and gym  as a physician very knowledgeable about  the issues I think will be present our  case well and I'm sure the Conservatives  will do their best as well well there's  been a great deal of surprise because  most people wouldn't see Bernie Sanders  and I coming to an agreement on anything  and and so there's been your typical  harassment by some members in a friendly  way but most people have been very  supportive of the idea that there's been  some concern over special orders that go  on  after regular session is over that maybe  they should be limited others want to  argue that that we should keep them but  we want to do is to make special orders  more meaningful and that we think that  bringing the debates to the to the House  floor can help do them the moderator  would be another member of Congress  there would be an agreement between  their group and ours as to who the  moderator would be and who the  timekeeper would be but we have to do it  within the structure of the current  special orders and so that time is  allotted to a member and that member in  effect would have to be the moderator  and yield time to each side and and  organize the debate are we likely to see  a many members sitting in the chairs of  watching the debate I'm not sure that at  first we're going to see a lot of people  on the House floor during these but we  believe if they're successful if they're  done right if we have the right issues  and the right members involved that over  the next few months that these debates  can grow and become more of an event  hopefully on a weekly basis here in  Congress and one last question it's no  secret that the Democratic leadership is  talking about what they're calling  oxford style debates when the floor the  house possibly what is your reaction and  how does this fit into that well we  looked at the possibility of other  debate formats the lincoln-douglas  format is is pretty well structured with  a limited number of people on each side  and we think that within an hour you can  get to the heart of a question with  oxford style debates anybody sitting in  the room is entitled to to involve  themselves in the debate and we think  that that could get out of hand it could  be rather ruckus and and we're not sure  that the question then can be fully  explored and that's the whole idea here  can we fully explore a question in a  specific amount of time and so we  settled on the lincoln-douglas style and  it's going to be incumbent upon us to  structure questions that are narrow  enough yet broad enough to be discussed  in an hour period of time Congressman  John Boehner thank you thank you
 

